D in social psychology and he started  doing research on prejudice and conflict  at the time many psychologists assumed  that extreme cases of prejudice resulted  from certain personality variables that  were associated with a general tendency  to be prejudiced and dogmatic in  particular a great deal of research had  been conducted on the authoritarian  personality which among other things  involves the tendency to be highly  prejudiced but Taj fell had seen  firsthand how themeing Lee ordinary  Germans had developed extreme prejudices  against the Jews so he set out to  understand the social and psychological  processes that can lead ordinary people  to become frigid Asst taj fel believed  that the first step in prejudice and  discrimination is the simple act of  distinguishing us from them so he was  particularly interested in the processes  by which people categorize themselves  and other people into groups people seem  to have a natural tendency the people  put people into categories and after we  put them into categories we start  thinking of everybody in that category  as being pretty much the same and we  often begin to hold negative attitudes  toward them well to study how this  process happens Taj fell planned to  bring people into a psychological  laboratory and assign them to one of two  groups and he wanted to assign them to  these two groups on the basis of  criteria  that were so unimportant and so  arbitrary that people wouldn't show the  normal biases and prejudices that we see  in real world groups and then with those  artificial minimal groups as a baseline  Taj fell planned to add factors to the  situation that would lead the members of  each group to become prejudiced toward  the other group starting with groups  that were so artificial and random that  prejudiced toward the other groups who  wouldn't arise he could then study what  made people discriminate against other  groups well it was a great experimental  strategy but it didn't work and it  didn't work because Taj Ville couldn't  find a basis for assigning people into  groups that was so random and so  arbitrary that the groups did not begin  to show patterns of in-group favoritism  and out group discrimination just  putting people into two different groups  even when they were assigned to groups  in a random or arbitrary or meaningless  way was enough to get the ball rolling  imagine that you're in one of these  studies we take a group of people all of  them pretty much alike and we randomly  put them in Group A or group B there's  no real difference between the groups we  just assigned people to the two groups  at random or on the basis of some  trivial difference but once they're in  their groups people start thinking that  their group is better than the other  group and they start behaving in ways  that benefit their own group and  disadvantage the other group and people  show this in-group favoritism even if  they don't know the members of the other  group and even if they don't expect to  interact with them in the future  just being a member of a group leads  people to identify with and prefer their  own group develop negative views of the  other group and favor their own group  when dividing up rewards with these  minimal group effects as a starting  point researchers have taken on the look  what makes prejudice and conflict  between groups even worse one approach  to understanding prejudice and conflict  called realistic conflict theory  proposes that discrimination and  conflict arise when groups are in  competition for some scarce resource  so once two groups start competing  against one another whether for  important things such as jobs or land or  relatively unimportant things such as  winning a contest or a football game  prejudiced and negative behaviors  intensify one research demonstration of  this effect was a well known study  conducted by Moussa fer Sharif and his  colleagues 22 11 and 12 year old boys  were recruited for a summer camp at  robbers cave State Park in Oklahoma the  boys were all quite similar all were  white kids from middle-class Protestant  backgrounds none of the boys knew each  other before the study but once they  showed up at robbers cave park for camp  they were randomly assigned to one of  two groups the boys chose names for  their groups the Eagles and the Rattlers  and they made group t-shirts and flags  but during the first phase of the study  they didn't know that there was another  group of boys at the camp but then after  giving the boys time to get to know the  other members of their own group Sherif  arranged for the two groups to begin to  compete with each other they played  baseball tug of war other games as the  competition began the Eagles and the  Rattlers quickly became suspicious of  each other at first the boys expressed  their hostility only verbally they  taunted the members of the other group  called each other names but as time wore  on the conflict escalated the Eagles  burned the Rattlers flag so the Rattlers  retaliated by ransacking the Eagles  cabin and stealing the Eagles property  as time wore on the groups became so  aggressive toward each other that the  researchers had to keep them separated  so nobody would get hurt  when the researchers ask the boys to  rate the members of their groups they  showed extreme strong in-group  favoritism raiding their own group much  more positively than the other group now  keep in mind that these were essentially  identical groups of well-adjusted boys  who had no reason whatsoever to dislike  each other at least initially the only  reason that they started acting this way  was that they were members of two groups  that were in competition with each other  so merely seeing oneself as a member of  a group can start these processes  rolling and competition can fuel them  but why why do we jump so quickly from  being a member of a group to developing  negative reactions to other groups with  which we compete to answer that question  let's think about why people deform  groups at all belonging to a group can  offer many benefits being in a group  gives you people to help you when you  need it and allows you to cooperate with  other people on tasks that would be  impossible for one person to do alone  members of a group can pool their  resources so that everyone has more  available to them than any one person  would have by him or herself and of  course groups provide opportunities for  social interaction companionship support  but joining up with other people is  always a bit of a risk being in a group  is beneficial only if the other members  of the group cooperate and reciprocate  and treat us well it's very easy to be  taken advantage of when other people  don't do their share because of the risk  of being taken advantage of people don't  try to forge cooperative relationships  with everybody they meet you don't go to  the mall and just pick people at random  to form groups because you don't know  whether you can trust them but forming a  group allows people to obtain the  benefits of cooperative relationships  while minimizing the possible costs  because most groups enforce rules for  how the members should treat each other  that's not to say we don't get taken  advantage of by people in our groups of  course we do but it happens less within  cohesive groups than if we were working  with a group of random people who  weren't members of an identifiable group  by joining groups selectively people can  obtain the advantages of cooperating  with other people while minimizing the  risks of being taken advantage of so by  defining who's in and who's out of a  particular group root boundaries tell us  who is most likely to be cooperative and  helpful and trustworthy all other things  being equal we can usually trust the  average member of the groups to which we  belong more than we can trust and rely  on the members of other groups of course  many of the people who are not members  of our groups are perfectly nice  trustworthy people but we don't know  which ones and many people who are not  in our groups have no incentive to treat  us well or if they are members of  competing groups they may be motivated  to work against us so the safest  solution is for people to assume that  anybody who's not in their group might  not be trustworthy  voilá that single somewhat reasonable  tactic for protecting ourselves can lead  us to view other groups with suspicion  and distrust  it might how it not always be nice and  it can lead to prejudice conflict and  other bad outcomes but it does make a  certain amount of psychological sense a  central feature of the prejudice and  conflict that develops between groups  involves biases that favor members of  one's own group and discriminate against  members of other groups even in Tosh  Fell's minimal group studies in which  people were assigned to groups  arbitrarily and had never met the other  members of either group people viewed  their own group more favorably  and made decisions that benefitted their  own group it's useful to think of this  as two distinct biases a bias to favor  one's own group and a bias to be against  another group these two biases often go  together but not necessarily I might be  biased to see my group in very favorable  terms and want good things for my group  but that doesn't necessarily mean that I  view your group negatively or want bad  things to happen to your group I might  think that your group is just fine and I  wish it all the best even though I still  think that my group is better and I'm  more invested in being sure that things  go well for my group than for yours of  course I could hate your group and hope  that bad things happen to it and I might  even act in a prejudicial or antisocial  way toward people from your group but  the more general pattern is for people  to strongly favor their own group but  not necessarily wish harm on other  groups in-group favoritism affects our  behavior more than out-group derogation  and antagonism to make things worse  research shows that groups of people are  less cooperative and more competitive  than individuals are that is if we take  10 people and we measure how each of  them responds as individuals to members  of other groups they will be more  cooperative and less competitive and if  we put them together as a group to  respond to other groups social  psychologists call this the inter  individual intergroup discontinuity  effect there are many historical  examples of individual members of groups  that are in conflict who get along just  fine as individuals but when those  individuals are back in their own groups  they are prejudiced and aggressive  toward the other group there are many  stories of soldiers from warring armies  sitting down to chat and have a  cigarette or before returning to their  own sides or stories of political  leaders of opposing nations who develop  amicable and cooperative  personal relationships with each other  sometimes people who hate some other  racial or ethnic group have personal  friends who are members of that group as  individuals they get along fine but they  are prejudiced against each others group  the inter individual intergroup  discontinuity effect is an impediment to  groups getting along reasonable non  prejudiced people who can get along with  members of the other group as  individuals change somehow when they get  into their group researchers who have  studied this effect have uncovered a  number of reasons for it the first is  that people are naturally more  suspicious and afraid of groups than  they are of individual people when we  think of having a conflict with a single  person it often seems manageable but  when we think of having the same  conflict with a group of people we view  the threat as more serious so when we  get together in a group to talk about  problems with another group we construe  the situation as more threatening and  more antagonistic and maybe it is  because the other group is likely to be  more antagonistic than the individual  members of the group would be but in any  case groups are more suspicious and less  conciliatory than individuals are on top  of that as individuals most of us don't  like to think of ourselves as unfair or  greedy or competitive or prejudiced so  we usually try to treat other people  with civility and cooperation even if we  don't like them but interacting in a  group of people encourages a competitive  mindset something about being in a group  leads people to respond more  competitively and antagonistic aliy and  once people start working as a group new  norms kick in norms that encourage them  urge them to act in ways that benefit  the group which sometimes requires  working against other groups  another bias that creeps into judgments  of our own and other groups involves the  fact that we tend to think that members  of other groups are more similar to each  other than they really are one  consequence of this is the out-group  homogeneity effect the idea that people  generalize the behavior of one member of  the out group to other members of the  out group and then to the group as a  whole if I have a bias to think that the  members of your group are mostly all  alike and I see one member being  dishonest and I'm likely to conclude  both that all of the members of your  group are dishonest and in fact that you  belong to a dishonest group I don't do  that with my own group seeing that one  member of my group is dishonest doesn't  lead me to conclude that my whole group  is bad as we have seen it doesn't take  much for these in-group and out-group  biases to emerge just put people into  groups and had a little competition over  political power or economic resources or  moral wild ideals or which group is the  best at something and these patterns  start to emerge and given how little it  takes to create prejudice discrimination  and conflict it's no wonder that people  act so badly when there's something  truly important at stake when groups are  in a win lose or zero-sum relationship  all of these effects become stronger  it's no wonder that conflicts such as  the israeli-palestinian conflict seem so  intractable prejudice and conflict also  intensify when people feel that their  group is disadvantaged relative to other  groups doesn't matter much how well the  group is doing in an objective sense  when members of a group think that their  situation is worse in comparison to  other groups they experienced  dissatisfaction and frustration they're  more likely to derogate members of other  groups and they're more likely to behave  aggressively now these reactions might  be reasonable  if the advantaged successful group then  something to hold the other group down  but we see this effect even when the  more advantaged group isn't in any way  responsible for the inequality between  the groups  just feeling less powerful or in a  disadvantaged position causes people to  show more prejudice this is why it's so  important to minimize political and  economic inequalities if we want groups  in society to stop fighting with each  other but prejudice and conflict don't  necessarily require competition for  scarce resources prejudice and  discrimination are also fed by the  perception that another group doesn't  share the values the attitudes the moral  standards of one's own group so for an  exit for example groups in a society  that appear to violate basic social  values tend to be victims of prejudice  and discrimination they don't need to be  doing anything illegal or hurting other  groups just behaving in ways that go  against other people's values and  traditions can create these divisions so  many Americans are prejudiced against  gays and lesbians and against atheists  and against people who have many tattoos  and body piercings  but none of these groups directly hurts  or disadvantages anybody the prejudices  against them are due solely to the fact  that their beliefs or behaviors differ  from the mainstream so prejudices arise  not only in response to important  outcomes such as whether you took my job  or you took my land but also to symbolic  threats to things that we value other  animals will fight when something  important is at stake food territory  mates protecting offspring but human  beings are the only species that gets  into conflicts and starts wars over  ideas and values  so prejudice and conflict arise easily  and naturally when certain conditions  are met as we have seen some of those  conditions involve how people think  about their own and other groups  especially when they're competing for  resources but to understand prejudice we  should also look at what individual  people get out of being prejudiced  social psychologists suggest that  prejudice can provide desirable  psychological outcomes for some people  for example prejudice against members of  another group can make people feel  better about themselves  research shows for example that people  who feel insecure about their own  abilities and personal characteristics  judge other people more harshly that may  be why whites from lower socioeconomic  backgrounds tend to be more prejudiced  against members of minority groups than  upper-class whites our research also  shows that people who have suffered a  blow to their self-esteem are more  likely to discriminate against others  than people who just experienced a boost  to their self-esteem presumably feeling  superior to other people can help people  feel better about themselves people who  have the greatest desire to feel good  about themselves either because they're  in a low status social group or because  they have suffered a momentary setback  are more likely to put other people down  you might be thinking that everything  that I've said so far paints a pretty  bleak picture if people develop  prejudices and conflicts so easily and  these reactions are maintained by biases  of various kinds then how can we ever  get groups to get along  it seems built into the nature of the  human psyche to dislike and distrust  other groups well there's a bit of truth  to that so efforts to break down walls  between groups have to counteract  people's natural tendency to build and  defend those walls but it's not hopeless  researchers and the social sciences have  spent a great deal of effort trying to  understand help fred judas develops and  how we can overcome it the first thing  that research has shown is that simply  throwing members of opposing groups  together is not usually an effective  strategy it might seem that if we could  just get people from the two groups to  get to know each other as individuals  then they'd realize that they're just  all human after all and dropped some of  their biases but it doesn't work that  way  remember the study of the adolescent  boys at the summer camp when researchers  tried to bring the Eagles and the  Rattlers together just for some fun  activities their antagonism escalated in  these kinds of situations the groups  don't like or trust each other and  simply putting them together isn't going  to change their views of each other but  although simply bringing the groups  together to interact isn't likely to be  helpful it is possible to structure  their contact in ways that reduce  prejudice and conflict back in the 1950s  gordon allport described the conditions  under which group contact is most likely  to reduce negative feelings between  groups researchers have extended his  findings but he got the basics down  pretty well first the groups must have  equal status within the situation in  which they will have contact and  interact with each other even if the  groups differ in social status on the  outside in the situation in which they  are brought together they must have  equal status and power second the  situation must provide an opportunity  for the groups to cooperate towards some  common goal in the robbers cave study  that I described the Eagles and the  Rattlers showed a reduction in their  hostilities only after they worked  together toward common goals for example  the researchers arranged to have the  water to the camp cut off and the two  groups had to work together to find and  fix the problem  there must also be opportunities for  members of the two groups to become  personally acquainted  although mere contact is not enough once  they start working toward common goals  people need to start to see each other  as individuals with concerns interests  and problems that are similar to their  own and finally contact between the  groups is more likely to reduce  prejudice if some higher authority  supports the goal of bringing the groups  together in reducing the conflict the  higher authority could be a person such  as a group leader could be laws or  social customs that promote getting  along efforts to reduce prejudice are  more likely to succeed if they appear to  have legitimacy as we solve prejudice  and conflict arise when people  categorize themselves as members of  different groups but if seeing  themselves as members of different  groups feeds the development of  prejudice and discrimination then think  of what might happen if we can get  people to construe the group categories  differently what if people started to  see themselves as part of the same group  as having a common in group identity the  idea is that we can change the way  people categorize themselves from us  versus them to we this is the sort of  thing people are talking about when they  say that the whole world would suddenly  bond together if aliens attacked from  outer space then we're no longer  Americans and Germans and Nigerians and  Chinese and Brazilians suddenly were the  people of the earth working together as  one group and it would probably work a  good deal of research has shown that we  can reduce prejudice by getting members  of diverse groups to develop a shared  identity studies have looked at all  kinds of groups such as different ethnic  groups in a high school bankers from  different companies who have been thrown  together because of a merger the two  parts of a blended family that comes  together when parents remarry  in all cases once the members of the two  old groups started reconceptualizing  themselves as members of the same group  prejudice and conflict decline  unfortunately this doesn't stop the  process altogether when we bring two  groups together in this way by stressing  their common identity members of those  two groups will start seeing themselves  as one group but guess what now the  members of the new unified group may  start seeing themselves as different  from other groups that maybe they hadn't  thought much about before that might be  the most discouraging thing under the  right conditions we can help groups and  conflict come together but then the  process might be repeated with other  groups but understanding why divisions  between groups develop and the things  that can help bring them together is  certainly a good first step

